Done. `slugify` in slug.js is now:

```js
function slugify(text) {
  return (
    String(text)
      .toLowerCase()
      .replace(/[\s_]+/g, "-")
      .replace(/[^\p{L}\p{N}-]/gu, "")
      .replace(/-+/g, "-")
      .replace(/^-+|-+$/g, "") || "untitled"
  );
}
```

Steps map directly to the rules: lowercase → spaces/underscores to hyphens → strip non-letters/numbers/hyphens (Unicode-aware via `\p{L}\p{N}`, so accented letters survive) → collapse hyphen runs and trim → `"untitled"` if empty.

Verified: `node --test` — 5 pass, 0 fail. Tests untouched.

Done. `calc2.js` now uses `count` in `sumAll`:

```js
function sumAll(numbers) {
  let count = 0;
  for (const x of numbers) count += x;
  return count;
}
```

Verified with `node --test`: 1 test, 1 pass, 0 fail.

Fixed. After the finite-number check, `add` now verifies the result:

```js
const result = a + b;
if (!Number.isSafeInteger(result)) {
  throw new RangeError("result exceeds safe integer range");
}
```

- `Number.isSafeInteger(MAX_SAFE_INTEGER + 1)` is false → throws, message matches `/exceeds safe integer/`
- `RangeError` for the value-range violation (vs. `TypeError` for bad argument types)
- Updated the function comment to document the new behavior

`node --test`: 3 tests, all pass.

Fixed. `add` now has a second guard:

```js
if (Number.isInteger(a) && Number.isInteger(b) && !Number.isSafeInteger(a + b)) {
  throw new TypeError("integer sum exceeds safe integer range");
}
```

One deliberate choice: the guard applies only when both inputs are integers. A blanket `!Number.isSafeInteger(a + b)` check would also reject fractional sums like `add(0.1, 0.2)`, which the documented contract still allows. The function comment in sum.js was updated to record the new behavior.

Verified: `node --test` — 3 tests, 3 pass, 0 fail (including the new `test/overflow.test.js`).
